### 4. Draw 5 rounds, naming 5 distinct tasks — the payoff this whole thread
### exists to reach.
### Actual response: 200, body {"value":"01a00920-ae4f-7967-b9da-c9919aaed71d"}
POST {{baseUrl}}/draw-phase
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "competitionId": { "value": "{{f3kCompId}}" },
  "rounds": 5,
  "taskRefs": ["A", "B", "C", "D", "E"]
}

### 5. Confirm via GET /competition: five rounds, each carrying the named
### task in order, each one group of 6.
### Actual response in this run (abbreviated to the fields that matter):
###   round 1: task A, groups=[6]
###   round 2: task B, groups=[6]
###   round 3: task C, groups=[6]
###   round 4: task D, groups=[6]
###   round 5: task E, groups=[6]
GET {{baseUrl}}/competition?Id={{f3kCompId}}

### =====================================================================
### Part 2 — F3K: naming a repeated task where the rules require distinct
### tasks is refused. A second, separate competition — a competition can
### only be drawn once (drawPhase.alreadyDrawn), so this leg cannot reuse
### Part 1's competition.
### =====================================================================

### 6. Create a second competition adopting F3K.
### Actual response: 200, body {"value":"01a00920-f48b-7816-8ff5-cd659bb7e1b7"}
POST {{baseUrl}}/create-competition
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "F3K E2E Test — repeat leg",
  "location": "Taupo",
  "startDate": "2026-01-10",
  "endDate": "2026-01-12",
  "classContentHash": "{{f3kHash}}"
}

> {%
    client.global.set("f3kCompId2", JSON.parse(response.body).value);
%}

### 7. Register six competitors (abbreviated here to one shown; the run
### registered all six the same way as steps 3's block, against
### pilot-f3k-repeat-N@example.com).
POST {{baseUrl}}/register-person
Content-Type: application/json

{ "name": "Pilot F3K Repeat 1", "contact": { "email": "pilot-f3k-repeat-1@example.com" }, "club": null }

> {% client.global.set("f3kPersonId2", JSON.parse(response.body).value); %}

###
POST {{baseUrl}}/register-competitor
Content-Type: application/json

{ "competitionId": { "value": "{{f3kCompId2}}" }, "personId": { "value": "{{f3kPersonId2}}" } }

### 8. Draw naming the same task twice — F3K.10's "each a different task"
### is RequireDistinctTaskPerRound on the preliminary phase.
### Actual response in this run: 400
###   {"type":"https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc9110#section-15.5.1",
###    "title":"drawPhase.taskSelectionNotDistinct","status":400,
###    "detail":"This phase requires a different task every round; the
###    catalogue offers 13 distinct task(s).", ...}
### The message states the catalogue size, not the literal repeated code —
### a deliberate choice (Competition.cs's DrawPhase, WI-2's own note: "a
### second code for the pigeonhole case would be a distinction without a
### difference at the API").
POST {{baseUrl}}/draw-phase
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "competitionId": { "value": "{{f3kCompId2}}" },
  "rounds": 2,
  "taskRefs": ["A", "A"]
}

### =====================================================================
### Part 3 — F5K: completes bind-parameter-e2e.http's abandoned F5K leg.
### Draw without binding minPerGroup -> parameterUnbound; bind; draw again
### naming tasks -> success. See this file's header for why F5K could not
### reach parameterUnbound before this thread.
### =====================================================================

### 9. Publish F5K's real class definition (reuses the existing
### publish-f5k-body.json from bind-parameter-e2e.http).
### Actual response: 200, body
###   "b064045339205123fe297b8068535270e3e6aff69cd9b780418856bcf6853986"
POST {{baseUrl}}/publish-class-definition
Content-Type: application/json

< ./publish-f5k-body.json

> {%
    client.global.set("f5kHash", JSON.parse(response.body));
%}

### 10. Create a competition adopting F5K.
### Actual response: 200, body {"value":"01a00921-0ff9-7c6f-9c01-93bf979f13aa"}
POST {{baseUrl}}/create-competition
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "F5K E2E Test",
  "location": "Taupo",
  "startDate": "2026-01-10",
  "endDate": "2026-01-12",
  "classContentHash": "{{f5kHash}}"
}

> {%
    client.global.set("f5kCompId", JSON.parse(response.body).value);
%}

### 11. Register five people + competitors (F5K's minPerGroup will be bound
### to 5 below). Run 1..5, abbreviated here to one shown; the actual run
### registered all five against pilot-f5k-N@example.com.
POST {{baseUrl}}/register-person
Content-Type: application/json

{ "name": "Pilot F5K 1", "contact": { "email": "pilot-f5k-1@example.com" }, "club": null }

> {% client.global.set("f5kPersonId", JSON.parse(response.body).value); %}

###
POST {{baseUrl}}/register-competitor
Content-Type: application/json

{ "competitionId": { "value": "{{f5kCompId}}" }, "personId": { "value": "{{f5kPersonId}}" } }

### 12. Attempt the draw with minPerGroup unbound.
### Actual response in this run: 400
###   {"type":"https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc9110#section-15.5.1",
###    "title":"drawPhase.parameterUnbound","status":400,
###    "detail":"Round 1 ('A'): Parameter 'minPerGroup' has no binding in
###    the provided bindings dictionary.", ...}
### THE FINDING: unlike the earlier bind-parameter-e2e.http run (which
### stopped at drawPhase.unsupportedRoundComposition before this parameter
### check was ever reached), F5K now reaches parameterUnbound — catalogue
### choice was the only thing standing between it and this check.
POST {{baseUrl}}/draw-phase
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "competitionId": { "value": "{{f5kCompId}}" },
  "rounds": 3,
  "taskRefs": ["A", "B", "C"]
}

### 13. Bind minPerGroup to 5.
### Actual response: 200, body {"value":"01a00921-0ff9-7c6f-9c01-93bf979f13aa"}
POST {{baseUrl}}/bind-parameter
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "competitionRef": { "value": "{{f5kCompId}}" },
  "parameterName": "minPerGroup",
  "value": { "kind": "Number", "number": 5 },
  "by": "CD Jane"
}

### 14. Draw again, naming tasks.
### Actual response: 200, body {"value":"01a00921-0ff9-7c6f-9c01-93bf979f13aa"}
### — success, where the unbound attempt in step 12 failed for the same
### competition and rounds, the one difference being step 13's bind.
POST {{baseUrl}}/draw-phase
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "competitionId": { "value": "{{f5kCompId}}" },
  "rounds": 3,
  "taskRefs": ["A", "B", "C"]
}

### 15. Confirm via GET /competition: three rounds, each carrying the named
### task in order, each one group of exactly 5 (the bound value).
### Actual response in this run (abbreviated to the fields that matter):
###   round 1: task A, groups=[5]
###   round 2: task B, groups=[5]
###   round 3: task C, groups=[5]
GET {{baseUrl}}/competition?Id={{f5kCompId}}
